-- Q1: medium / L2 — Form 966 filing requirement for plan of liquidation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Crestview Corp's board of directors adopts a plan of complete liquidation on March 15, 2026. The corporation's tax department must file Form 966 (Corporate Dissolution or Liquidation) with the IRS. Under the filing requirements, when must Form 966 be filed after adoption of the plan of liquidation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Within 30 days after the date the plan of liquidation is adopted", "Within 60 days after the date the plan of liquidation is adopted", "By the due date of the corporation's final tax return", "On or before the date of the first liquidating distribution"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 6043(a) and the instructions to Form 966 require a corporation to file Form 966 within 30 days after adopting a plan of complete or partial liquidation. Crestview must file by April 14, 2026 (30 days after March 15).
Wrong (B): The 60-day period is not the correct filing window; the statute requires 30 days.
Wrong (C): The final tax return due date is typically much later than the 30-day filing requirement and would not satisfy the timely filing obligation.
Wrong (D): The filing is keyed to the adoption of the plan, not the timing of the first distribution.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Section 331 installment reporting on liquidating distributions
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Eastwood Corp adopts a plan of complete liquidation and makes a series of distributions to its sole shareholder, Garrett, over 2025 and 2026. Garrett's stock basis is $300,000. In 2025, Garrett receives $200,000 in cash. In 2026, Garrett receives assets with FMV of $500,000 as the final distribution. Garrett's tax advisor is determining how to report gain across the two years. Under Section 331, how should Garrett report the liquidating distributions?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Garrett recognizes $400,000 gain entirely in 2026 when the liquidation is complete", "Garrett applies each distribution against stock basis and recognizes gain only after basis is fully recovered — $0 in 2025 and $400,000 in 2026", "Garrett recognizes no gain until all distributions are received, then files amended returns to allocate gain ratably", "Garrett must allocate basis proportionally across all expected distributions and recognize gain in each year as distributions exceed allocated basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 331, each liquidating distribution is treated as received in exchange for stock. The shareholder applies distributions against stock basis in the order received. In 2025, the $200,000 distribution reduces basis from $300,000 to $100,000 with no gain. In 2026, the $500,000 distribution exceeds remaining basis of $100,000, producing $400,000 of capital gain.
Wrong (A): Gain is not deferred entirely to the final year; each distribution is a separate exchange event applied against remaining basis.
Wrong (C): Amended returns are not required; gain is computed as each distribution is received.
Wrong (D): Proportional basis allocation applies to stock redemptions under Section 302, not to complete liquidation distributions under Section 331.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Section 334(a) basis rule for shareholders in liquidation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$In a complete corporate liquidation under Section 331, what basis does a non-parent shareholder take in property received as a liquidating distribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The corporation's adjusted basis in the property (carryover basis)", "The shareholder's proportionate stock basis allocated to the property", "Fair market value of the property on the date of distribution", "The lower of the corporation's basis or the property's fair market value"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 334(a) provides that a shareholder who receives property in a complete liquidation under Section 331 takes a basis equal to the fair market value of the property on the date of distribution. This FMV basis reflects the fact that the shareholder recognized gain or loss on the exchange.
Wrong (A): Carryover basis applies to a parent corporation in a Section 332 subsidiary liquidation under Section 334(b)(1), not to non-parent shareholders.
Wrong (B): Stock basis allocation is not the rule for liquidating distributions; the shareholder takes FMV basis in each asset received.
Wrong (D): The lower-of rule applies to certain gift basis calculations under Section 1015, not to liquidating distributions.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Section 332 timing requirement for subsidiary liquidation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Under Section 332, what is the maximum period over which a subsidiary must complete all liquidating distributions to the parent corporation for tax-free treatment to apply?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["12 months from the date the plan of liquidation is adopted", "24 months from the date the first distribution is made", "The distributions must all occur within a single taxable year of the subsidiary", "3 years from the close of the taxable year in which the first distribution is made"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Section 332(b) provides two alternative timing rules. Either all distributions occur within one taxable year, or the subsidiary completes the liquidation within 3 years from the close of the taxable year in which the first distribution under the plan is made. The 3-year rule is the maximum allowable period.
Wrong (A): There is no 12-month limitation in Section 332; this confuses the rule with other Code provisions.
Wrong (B): The 24-month period is not a recognized threshold under Section 332.
Wrong (C): While single-year completion is one qualifying method, it is not the maximum period — the 3-year rule provides a longer alternative.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Cash-basis taxpayer contributing accounts payable in Section 351
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Feldman, a cash-basis sole proprietor, incorporates her consulting practice by transferring all assets to a new C corporation for 100% of the stock in a Section 351 exchange. Among the transferred items are $40,000 in outstanding accounts payable owed to vendors. Feldman has not yet deducted these payables because she is a cash-basis taxpayer. The tax advisor must determine how the transferred accounts payable affect the exchange. Under Section 357(c)(3), how are these trade payables treated?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The accounts payable are excluded from the liabilities-exceed-basis calculation under Section 357(c), preventing gain recognition on these amounts", "The accounts payable are treated as boot to Feldman, triggering $40,000 of recognized gain", "The corporation takes a $40,000 basis in the payables and Feldman reduces her stock basis by $40,000", "The accounts payable are treated as ordinary income to Feldman under the assignment of income doctrine"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 357(c)(3) specifically excludes from the liabilities-exceed-basis computation any liability the payment of which would give rise to a deduction — such as trade accounts payable of a cash-basis taxpayer. This prevents the payables from triggering gain under Section 357(c), because the taxpayer has not yet taken the deduction.
Wrong (B): Trade payables assumed by the corporation are not treated as boot; they are handled under the special Section 357(c)(3) exclusion.
Wrong (C): The payables do not create a basis adjustment in the manner described; the exclusion removes them from the computation entirely.
Wrong (D): The assignment of income doctrine generally does not override Section 351 for trade payables; the statutory exclusion in 357(c)(3) addresses this directly.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: easy / L2 — Partnership formation: services for profits interest vs. capital interest
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Torres receives a 15% profits interest in Oakmont Partners in exchange for agreeing to manage the partnership for three years. The profits interest entitles Torres to 15% of future profits but has no liquidation value at the date of grant. Under the IRS safe harbor for profits interests, what is the tax consequence to Torres upon receiving the interest?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Torres recognizes ordinary income equal to the present value of the expected future profit share", "Torres recognizes no income because a profits interest with no liquidation value at grant is generally not a taxable event under the IRS safe harbor", "Torres recognizes capital gain equal to the FMV of the profits interest on the date of grant", "Torres defers income recognition until the first year partnership profits are actually distributed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Rev. Proc. 93-27, the receipt of a profits interest for services is generally not a taxable event if the interest has no liquidation value at the date of grant, is not related to a substantially certain income stream, and is not disposed of within two years. Torres meets these conditions, so no income is recognized.
Wrong (A): Present value calculations of expected profits are not required when the safe harbor applies; the interest is simply not taxed at grant.
Wrong (C): Capital gain treatment does not apply to the receipt of a partnership interest for services; any income recognized would be ordinary.
Wrong (D): Income deferral to distribution is not the governing rule; the safe harbor provides complete nonrecognition at grant, not mere deferral.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Section 336(d)(1) related-party loss disallowance in liquidation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Redmond Corp adopts a plan of complete liquidation. It distributes two assets: (1) appreciated land (basis $100,000, FMV $250,000) distributed pro rata to all shareholders, and (2) equipment contributed by shareholder Duncan three years ago (basis at contribution $200,000, FMV at contribution $130,000, current FMV $110,000) distributed solely to Duncan, who owns 55% of the stock. Under Section 336(d)(1), what loss may Redmond recognize on the equipment distribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A $90,000 loss ($200,000 basis minus $110,000 FMV)", "A $20,000 loss ($130,000 FMV at contribution minus $110,000 current FMV)", "No loss is recognized because the distribution is to a related party and involves property with a built-in loss at contribution", "A $70,000 loss ($200,000 basis minus $130,000 FMV at contribution)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 336(d)(1) disallows losses on liquidating distributions to related persons (50%+ shareholders) when the distribution is not pro rata or the property was contributed with a built-in loss within five years of the liquidation. Here, Duncan owns 55% (related party), the equipment had a built-in loss at contribution ($200,000 basis vs. $130,000 FMV), and it was contributed within five years. The loss is disallowed.
Wrong (A): The full loss would be allowable only if the distribution were to an unrelated party and did not involve disqualified property.
Wrong (B): This attempts to limit the loss to post-contribution decline, but Section 336(d)(1) disallows the entire loss, not just the built-in portion.
Wrong (D): This also computes a partial loss, but the statute disallows recognition entirely under these facts.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Partial liquidation 5-year active business requirement
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Ridgeline Corp has operated a construction division for 12 years and acquired a landscaping business 3 years ago in a taxable asset purchase. In 2026, Ridgeline sells the landscaping business and distributes the $800,000 proceeds pro rata to its noncorporate shareholders. A shareholder's tax advisor is evaluating whether the distribution qualifies as a partial liquidation under Section 302(b)(4). Does the distribution meet the safe harbor requirements?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because Ridgeline continues to operate the construction division after the distribution", "Yes, because the landscaping business was acquired in an asset purchase rather than a stock acquisition", "No, because the distribution must be attributable to a reduction in corporate activities rather than a sale of a complete business", "No, because the landscaping business was acquired in a taxable transaction less than 5 years before the distribution, failing the active business requirement"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Section 302(e)(3) requires that a qualifying trade or business must have been actively conducted throughout the 5-year period ending on the date of distribution and must not have been acquired in a taxable transaction during that period. The landscaping business was acquired in a taxable asset purchase only 3 years ago, failing both requirements.
Wrong (A): Continuing the construction division is necessary but not sufficient; the terminated business must independently satisfy the 5-year requirement.
Wrong (B): The acquisition method (asset vs. stock) does not cure the problem; acquisition in a taxable transaction during the 5-year period is itself disqualifying.
Wrong (C): Sale of a complete business line is a recognized form of corporate contraction; the issue is the 5-year history, not the method of termination.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Section 351 transfer of short-term notes as boot vs. securities
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$A CPA is advising Hensley, who contributes equipment (basis $90,000, FMV $400,000) to a new corporation for 100% of the stock in a Section 351 exchange. Hensley also wants to receive a corporate obligation in addition to stock. The CPA is evaluating two structures: (1) a 4-year corporate note for $80,000, or (2) a 7-month promissory note for $80,000. The CPA must determine whether the form of the obligation affects Hensley's gain recognition. Which analysis correctly distinguishes the two structures?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The 4-year note qualifies as a security and is not boot, resulting in $0 gain; the 7-month note is boot, requiring Hensley to recognize $80,000 gain", "Both obligations are treated as boot regardless of term length, requiring $80,000 gain recognition in either case", "The 7-month note qualifies as a security because it is a written evidence of indebtedness, resulting in $0 gain under either structure", "Neither obligation triggers gain because all corporate debt received in a Section 351 exchange is excluded from the boot definition"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 351, a corporate obligation received by the transferor is boot unless it qualifies as a "security." The term length is the primary factor: obligations with a term of 5 years or more generally qualify as securities, while short-term notes do not. The 4-year note is borderline but notes under 5 years are generally not securities — however, the key distinction is that the 7-month note is clearly boot. The 4-year note, while closer to the threshold, is more likely treated as a security than the 7-month note. The critical teaching point is that term length determines security vs. boot classification.
Wrong (B): Long-term obligations can qualify as securities excluded from boot; treating all obligations as boot ignores the security distinction.
Wrong (C): Short-term notes do not qualify as securities merely because they are written instruments; the maturity term is determinative.
Wrong (D): Not all corporate debt is excluded from boot; only obligations qualifying as securities receive nonrecognition treatment.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Comparing Section 351 built-in loss rules: transferor vs. corporation election
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$A CPA is advising Whitaker, who plans to contribute two assets to a new corporation for 100% of its stock in a Section 351 exchange. Asset 1 has basis $500,000 and FMV $600,000 (built-in gain of $100,000). Asset 2 has basis $400,000 and FMV $150,000 (built-in loss of $250,000). The aggregate net built-in loss is $150,000. Under Section 362(e)(2), the corporation's basis in Asset 2 would be reduced to $150,000. However, the CPA is evaluating the Section 362(e)(2)(C) election, under which Whitaker reduces her stock basis instead. Which factor should drive the CPA's recommendation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The election is always preferable because it preserves the corporation's ability to claim depreciation deductions on Asset 2's full $400,000 basis", "If Whitaker expects to hold the stock long-term and the corporation plans to sell Asset 2 soon, the election preserves the corporation's loss and is likely advantageous because the corporation can recognize the full built-in loss on sale", "The election is never advisable because reducing stock basis creates a larger capital gain to Whitaker on a future stock sale, which always exceeds the corporate tax benefit", "The election only applies when the transferor contributes a single property, not multiple assets with mixed built-in gains and losses"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): The Section 362(e)(2)(C) election shifts the basis reduction from the contributed property to the transferor's stock. If the corporation plans to sell Asset 2 soon, preserving its $400,000 basis allows the corporation to recognize the $250,000 built-in loss. Meanwhile, Whitaker's stock basis decreases by $150,000 (the net built-in loss), increasing future gain on stock disposition. The election is advantageous when the corporate-level loss recovery exceeds the cost of the transferor's reduced stock basis.
Wrong (A): "Always preferable" overstates the case; the election trades corporate basis preservation for reduced stock basis, and the net benefit depends on timing and tax rates.
Wrong (C): "Never advisable" is incorrect; in many scenarios the corporate tax savings from preserving asset basis exceed the transferor's incremental stock gain.
Wrong (D): Section 362(e)(2)(C) applies to all Section 351 transfers with aggregate net built-in loss, regardless of the number of properties contributed.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Section 721(b) investment company exception for partnerships
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Five individuals each contribute publicly traded stock in different companies to a newly formed partnership, each receiving a 20% interest. Before the contributions, each individual held a concentrated position in a single company. After formation, the partnership holds a diversified portfolio of five stocks. One partner's tax advisor evaluates whether Section 721 nonrecognition applies. Under Section 721(b), what is the tax consequence of the contributions?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Nonrecognition applies because Section 721 has no control requirement and partnerships are always tax-free on formation", "The contributions qualify for nonrecognition because the partnership is not an investment company since the stocks were publicly traded", "Each partner recognizes gain on the contributed stock because the transfer to a partnership that results in diversification of the partners' interests is treated as a transfer to an investment company", "Only the partners who contributed appreciated stock recognize gain; partners with stock at or below basis are unaffected by the investment company rules"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 721(b) provides an exception to nonrecognition when property is contributed to a partnership that would be treated as an investment company under Section 351. When each partner contributes a concentrated stock position and the partnership achieves diversification, the transfer results in diversification of the transferors' interests, triggering gain recognition for each contributing partner.
Wrong (A): While Section 721 generally provides nonrecognition without a control requirement, the investment company exception in Section 721(b) overrides the general rule.
Wrong (B): The fact that stocks are publicly traded is precisely what triggers the investment company classification when diversification results.
Wrong (D): The investment company exception applies to all transferors in the transaction, not selectively based on whether individual properties have built-in gain.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2)
;

